WEBVTT

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:12.800
In this video a picture becomes an embroidery design: a toadstool, the kind you find online or paint yourself.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.043
At the top, switch to the Picture mode.

00:00:17.043 --> 00:00:21.475
Then simply drag your picture into the box on the left.

00:00:21.475 --> 00:00:24.940
A few seconds later it is already stitched.

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:29.084
Above the canvas you switch views.

00:00:29.084 --> 00:00:36.619
“Prepared” shows what heatstitch makes of the picture: a few smooth areas of colour.

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:41.553
But look at the stem: it is grey, just like the shadow.

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:45.458
Six colours are too few here.

00:00:45.458 --> 00:00:48.827
With nine, the stem gets its beige.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:58.117
In return, the gradient on the cap now becomes two shades of red.

00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:05.293
One red is enough: click the arrow and merge it with red.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.440
I don't want to stitch the shadow, so I untick it.

00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:16.227
That leaves the red spot at the top right.

00:01:16.227 --> 00:01:20.272
Unticking red won't do, the cap would be gone.

00:01:20.272 --> 00:01:26.515
That's what the brush is for: Erase, one stroke over it, and it's gone.

00:01:26.515 --> 00:01:34.253
Now there are five thread colours again, as at the start, only this time the right ones.

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:42.935
Under “Stitches” you see the design, and with “As stitched” the thread shines in the light.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:48.008
On the right, heatstitch checks the stitches for your fabric.

00:01:48.008 --> 00:01:55.327
Two spots are a little dense; you look at them in the Density mode after taking it over.

00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:03.376
One click on “Use as design”, and your toadstool is an ordinary embroidery design.

00:02:03.376 --> 00:02:08.033
You can play it, check it and save it as a stitch file.

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:17.372
One last tip: photos turn out much better if an AI first turns them into a flat graphic.

00:02:17.372 --> 00:02:20.649
You copy the matching request here.

00:02:20.649 --> 00:02:26.453
And heatstitch takes over an SVG file shape by shape, exactly.
